{%- comment -%}
  Seoul Glow Lab — Sizing comparison (3 octagons at true relative scale)

  Three octagonal silhouettes drawn at exact relative scale on a flat
  paper plate. Tapping any size:
    • Lights up its portal (radial gradient + concentric rings)
    • Updates the detail panel (dimensions, use, price)
  Default selection is "L" (Large).

  Real specs from casadilumo.com (verified by Casa Design chat
  transcript):
    Small  · 11.8" / 30 cm  · $249
    Medium · 19.7" / 50 cm  · $299
    Large  · 27.6" / 70 cm  · $349  · POPULAR (per merchant: large size
                                       is the conversion target across
                                       every mirror PDP) · qualifies for
                                       free Mini Sunset Orb

  Plate is 80" × 40" (relative units). Centerlines at 13/36/64" so each
  mirror has clear gutter to its neighbor.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings
  assign default_size = s.default_size | default: 'L'
  assign mirror_shape = s.mirror_shape | default: 'octagon'

  comment
    All 5 mirror shapes ship in SQUARE FRAMES per Seoul Glow Lab's
    published spec — the inscribed shape (hex, triangle, etc.) sits
    inside a square bounding box that's what actually mounts on the
    wall. Customer-facing W × H is the frame's bounding box for every
    shape: H = W (square aspect). Confirmed by reading every mirror's
    PDP on casadilumo.com on 2026-05-09 — Hex Large = '27.5" × 27.5"',
    not the 27.5 × 23.9 I'd previously calculated from interior
    geometry.

    The polygon SVG points define the INSCRIBED shape (visible inner
    polygon), but the bounding box stays square — viewBox is 100×100
    and mirror_aspect = 1 across all non-circle shapes.
  endcomment
  comment
    tcx / tcy = the tunnel's vanishing point, i.e. the centre the nested
    rings scale toward. It is the shape's CENTROID, not the viewBox
    middle — for the triangle those differ by 14 units and nesting on the
    viewBox centre makes the rings drift out of the frame.
  endcomment
  case mirror_shape
    when 'hexagon-flat'
      assign poly_pts = '25,1 75,1 99,43.3 75,85.6 25,85.6 1,43.3'
      assign svg_viewbox = '0 0 100 86.6'
      assign mirror_aspect = '1.1547'
      assign tcx = 50.0
      assign tcy = 43.3
    when 'quadrilateral'
      assign poly_pts = '1,1 99,1 99,99 1,99'
      assign svg_viewbox = '0 0 100 100'
      assign mirror_aspect = '1'
      assign tcx = 50.0
      assign tcy = 50.0
    when 'triangle'
      assign poly_pts = '50,1 99,85.6 1,85.6'
      assign svg_viewbox = '0 0 100 86.6'
      assign mirror_aspect = '1.1547'
      assign tcx = 50.0
      assign tcy = 57.4
    when 'circle'
      assign is_circle = true
      assign svg_viewbox = '0 0 100 100'
      assign mirror_aspect = '1'
      assign tcx = 50.0
      assign tcy = 50.0
    when 'cube'
      comment
        Infinity Cube renders as a square (face-on view) at scale.
        Same poly as 'quadrilateral'; separate enum value so CSS can
        target `[data-casa-sz-shape="cube"]` if cube-specific styling
        (e.g. iridescent fill animation) is added later.
      endcomment
      assign poly_pts = '1,1 99,1 99,99 1,99'
      assign svg_viewbox = '0 0 100 100'
      assign mirror_aspect = '1'
      assign tcx = 50.0
      assign tcy = 50.0
    else
      assign poly_pts = '29.3,1 70.7,1 99,29.3 99,70.7 70.7,99 29.3,99 1,70.7 1,29.3'
      assign svg_viewbox = '0 0 100 100'
      assign mirror_aspect = '1'
      assign tcx = 50.0
      assign tcy = 50.0
  endcase

  comment
    Width × Height labels per size — square frame for every shape.
  endcomment
  assign hcm_s = '30'
  assign hcm_m = '50'
  assign hcm_l = '70'
  assign hin_s = '11.8'
  assign hin_m = '19.7'
  assign hin_l = '27.6'

  comment
    Depth (3rd dimension) — per-size, merchant-fillable via schema.
    Inches schema field stores JUST the number ('2.4') because storing
    the literal " character inside a data-* attribute breaks HTML
    parsing. The inch symbol is appended in the template after the
    span, same way W and H do it.
  endcomment
  assign depth_s_in = s.depth_s_in | default: '1.6'
  assign depth_m_in = s.depth_m_in | default: '2.0'
  assign depth_l_in = s.depth_l_in | default: '2.4'
  assign depth_s_cm = s.depth_s_cm | default: '4 cm'
  assign depth_m_cm = s.depth_m_cm | default: '5 cm'
  assign depth_l_cm = s.depth_l_cm | default: '6 cm'

  assign v_s = product.variants[0]
  assign v_m = product.variants[1]
  assign v_l = product.variants[2]

  comment
    ── BUY ROW ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The column was always designed as three groups — the comment above
    .casa-sz__spec says "SPEC (what you picked) -> CHOOSE (pick another)
    -> BUY" — but BUY was never built. Until now the shopper compared
    three sizes here and then had to scroll back up to the buy box to act
    on the comparison, which is the one moment they are most decided.

    Price is formatted in Liquid, never in JS, so Markets currency
    conversion is correct in all 110 currencies. It honours
    `currency_code_enabled_product_pages` for the same reason snippets/
    price.liquid does: this row sits on the same page as the main buy
    box, and two different money formats on one page reads as a bug.

    Per-size strings ride on the chips as data-* and the JS copies the
    selected one into the row. Rendering three hidden rows instead would
    mean three sources of truth for one line.
  endcomment
  assign use_currency = false
  if settings.currency_code_enabled_product_pages
    assign use_currency = true
  endif

  assign p_s = ''
  assign w_s = ''
  if v_s
    if use_currency
      assign p_s = v_s.price | money_with_currency | strip_html
    else
      assign p_s = v_s.price | money | strip_html
    endif
    if v_s.compare_at_price > v_s.price
      if use_currency
        assign w_s = v_s.compare_at_price | money_with_currency | strip_html
      else
        assign w_s = v_s.compare_at_price | money | strip_html
      endif
    endif
  endif

  assign p_m = ''
  assign w_m = ''
  if v_m
    if use_currency
      assign p_m = v_m.price | money_with_currency | strip_html
    else
      assign p_m = v_m.price | money | strip_html
    endif
    if v_m.compare_at_price > v_m.price
      if use_currency
        assign w_m = v_m.compare_at_price | money_with_currency | strip_html
      else
        assign w_m = v_m.compare_at_price | money | strip_html
      endif
    endif
  endif

  assign p_l = ''
  assign w_l = ''
  if v_l
    if use_currency
      assign p_l = v_l.price | money_with_currency | strip_html
    else
      assign p_l = v_l.price | money | strip_html
    endif
    if v_l.compare_at_price > v_l.price
      if use_currency
        assign w_l = v_l.compare_at_price | money_with_currency | strip_html
      else
        assign w_l = v_l.compare_at_price | money | strip_html
      endif
    endif
  endif

  comment
    ── WHICH SIZE THE STRIP OPENS ON ───────────────────────────────────
    The buy box wins, NOT the `default_size` setting.

    This strip used to open on Large regardless of what the buy box had
    selected. That was invisible while the strip showed no price — it is
    a comparison, and comparisons don't have to agree with anything. The
    moment it grew a price and an Add to cart it became a third opinion
    about what you are buying: measured on a bare Octamirror load, the
    buy box and the sticky bar both said Small €275,95 while the strip
    said Large €733,95.

    So the strip resolves its opening size from
    `selected_or_first_available_variant` — the same variant the buy box
    and the sticky bar render — which also makes a ?variant= deep link
    land on the right chip. `default_size` stays as the fallback for a
    product whose selected variant is not one of these three.

    Rendering the whole panel from this (not just the price) matters:
    the JS calls select() on init, so a server render fixed at Large
    would paint Large and then swap to Small on nearly every load.
  endcomment
  assign sel_v = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign init_size = default_size
  if sel_v
    if v_s and sel_v.id == v_s.id
      assign init_size = 'S'
    elsif v_m and sel_v.id == v_m.id
      assign init_size = 'M'
    elsif v_l and sel_v.id == v_l.id
      assign init_size = 'L'
    endif
  endif

  comment
    Per-size copy lives in Liquid so the server can paint whichever size
    is opening. The JS reads these off the chips (the same merge the
    depth values already use), which keeps ONE source of truth — before
    this, the Large string was written out in the markup AND again in
    the script's DATA table.
  endcomment
  assign use_s = 'Vanities, nooks, desks'
  assign use_m = 'Bedrooms, entryways, hallways'
  assign use_l = 'Above sofas, gaming rooms, lounges'

  case init_size
    when 'S'
      assign v_def = v_s
      assign p_def = p_s
      assign w_def = w_s
      assign init_name = 'Small'
      assign init_in = hin_s
      assign init_cm = hcm_s
      assign init_d_in = depth_s_in
      assign init_d_cm = depth_s_cm
      assign init_use = use_s
      assign init_pop = false
    when 'M'
      assign v_def = v_m
      assign p_def = p_m
      assign w_def = w_m
      assign init_name = 'Medium'
      assign init_in = hin_m
      assign init_cm = hcm_m
      assign init_d_in = depth_m_in
      assign init_d_cm = depth_m_cm
      assign init_use = use_m
      assign init_pop = false
    else
      assign v_def = v_l
      assign p_def = p_l
      assign w_def = w_l
      assign init_name = 'Large'
      assign init_in = hin_l
      assign init_cm = hcm_l
      assign init_d_in = depth_l_in
      assign init_d_cm = depth_l_cm
      assign init_use = use_l
      assign init_pop = true
  endcase

  unless v_def
    assign v_def = sel_v
    if v_def
      if use_currency
        assign p_def = v_def.price | money_with_currency | strip_html
      else
        assign p_def = v_def.price | money | strip_html
      endif
      assign w_def = ''
    endif
  endunless

  comment
    Default ON: a nil setting (template saved before this field existed)
    must read as true, so the six live mirror PDPs get the row without
    the merchant having to open each one. `s.show_atc == false` is the
    only thing that turns it off.
  endcomment
  assign show_atc = true
  if s.show_atc == false
    assign show_atc = false
  endif
  unless v_def
    assign show_atc = false
  endunless

  comment
    ── THE TUNNEL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Built from the real product photo, not from a guess. An infinity
    mirror is a half-silvered front over a fully mirrored back, so what
    you actually see is the LED outline repeating, shrinking toward a
    vanishing point — plus a straight SPOKE running from every frame
    vertex to the centre, which is the corner seam of that tunnel.

    The spokes are what sell it. Concentric rings alone read as a
    bullseye; the spokes are what make it read as depth. The old
    treatment was a single radial gradient with a white-hot centre,
    which is backwards twice over: no repetition, and the real core is
    the DENSEST part (rings crowding together), not a soft glow.

    Strokes are non-scaling so every reflection stays the same hairline
    width the way the real LED does — only opacity falls off with depth.
  endcomment
  assign plate_mode = s.plate_mode | default: 'three'


  assign ring_scales = '0.88,0.72,0.59,0.485,0.4,0.325,0.267,0.219,0.18,0.147,0.121,0.099,0.081' | split: ','
  assign spoke_outer = 0.88
  assign spoke_inner = 0.081

  capture tunnel_svg
    echo '<g class="casa-sz__tunnel" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="url(#casa-sz-led-' | append: section.id | append: ')">'
    unless is_circle
      assign verts = poly_pts | split: ' '
      for v in verts
        assign xy = v | split: ','
        assign vx = xy[0] | plus: 0.0
        assign vy = xy[1] | plus: 0.0
        assign ox = vx | minus: tcx | times: spoke_outer | plus: tcx
        assign oy = vy | minus: tcy | times: spoke_outer | plus: tcy
        assign ix = vx | minus: tcx | times: spoke_inner | plus: tcx
        assign iy = vy | minus: tcy | times: spoke_inner | plus: tcy
        echo '<line class="casa-sz__spoke" style="--i:' | append: forloop.index0 | append: ';--o:0.62" x1="'
        echo ox | append: '" y1="' | append: oy | append: '" x2="' | append: ix | append: '" y2="' | append: iy | append: '" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />'
      endfor
    endunless
    for k in ring_scales
      assign ri = forloop.index0
      assign fade = ri | times: 0.052
      assign ropacity = 1.0 | minus: fade
      if is_circle
        assign rr = 49.0 | times: k
        echo '<circle class="casa-sz__ring" style="--i:' | append: ri | append: ';--o:' | append: ropacity | append: '" cx="' | append: tcx | append: '" cy="' | append: tcy
        echo '" r="' | append: rr | append: '" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />'
      else
        echo '<polygon class="casa-sz__ring" style="--i:' | append: ri | append: ';--o:' | append: ropacity | append: '" points="' | append: poly_pts
        echo '" transform="translate(' | append: tcx | append: ' ' | append: tcy | append: ') scale(' | append: k
        echo ') translate(-' | append: tcx | append: ' -' | append: tcy | append: ')" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />'
      endif
    endfor
    echo '</g>'
  endcapture
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Per-instance stroke gradient. Defined once in a zero-size SVG and
  referenced by id from all three mirrors — a paint server resolves
  across SVG roots in the same document, so three copies were redundant.
  Vertical, because that is how the real RGB-IC ring reads in the
  product photography: the spectrum sweeps top-to-bottom across the
  whole tunnel rather than per-reflection.
{%- endcomment -%}
<svg width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" style="position:absolute">
  <defs>
    <linearGradient id="casa-sz-led-{{ section.id }}" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
      {%- if s.tunnel_palette == 'spectrum' -%}
        <stop offset="0%"   stop-color="#FF2D55" />
        <stop offset="22%"  stop-color="#F59E0B" />
        <stop offset="44%"  stop-color="#22C55E" />
        <stop offset="68%"  stop-color="{{ s.color_cyan   | default: '#F2FFA0' }}" />
        <stop offset="100%" stop-color="{{ s.color_purple | default: '#B0C3FA' }}" />
      {%- else -%}
        <stop offset="0%"   stop-color="{{ s.color_purple | default: '#B0C3FA' }}" />
        <stop offset="50%"  stop-color="{{ s.color_pink   | default: '#5E0A02' }}" />
        <stop offset="100%" stop-color="{{ s.color_cyan   | default: '#F2FFA0' }}" />
      {%- endif -%}
    </linearGradient>
  </defs>
</svg>

<section
  id="casa-sz-{{ section.id }}"
  class="casa-sz{% if s.layout_density == 'one_frame' %} casa-sz--compact{% endif %}"
  data-section-id="{{ section.id }}"
  data-default="{{ init_size }}"
  {%- comment -%}
    Routes ride on the root because the script below is a static
    "javascript" tag — Shopify serves it once for the whole theme and
    does NOT run Liquid inside it, so a routes.cart_add_url output written
    there would ship to the browser as that literal string. Locale-
    prefixed storefronts (/en-de/cart/add) depend on the real route, so
    hardcoding "/cart/add.js" is not a fallback either.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  data-cart-add-url="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}"
  data-cart-url="{{ routes.cart_url }}"
  style="
    --casa-sz-paper: {{ s.color_paper | default: '#FAFAF7' }};
    --casa-sz-ink:   {{ s.color_ink   | default: '#0A0A0F' }};
    --casa-sz-pink:  {{ s.color_pink  | default: '#5E0A02' }};
    --casa-sz-purple:{{ s.color_purple| default: '#B0C3FA' }};
    --casa-sz-cyan:  {{ s.color_cyan  | default: '#F2FFA0' }};
    --casa-sz-pt:    {{ s.padding_top    | default: 96 }}px;
    --casa-sz-pb:    {{ s.padding_bottom | default: 96 }}px;
  "
>
  <div class="casa-sz__cap">
    <div class="casa-sz__inner">
      {%- comment -%} LEFT — copy + selector {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Intro is its own grid item, NOT the head of the copy column. On a
        phone the running order has to be heading -> mirrors -> spec, and
        the mirrors cannot jump above the heading while they share a
        wrapper with it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="casa-sz__intro">
        {%- if s.kicker != blank -%}
          <p class="casa-sz__mono">{{ s.kicker }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if s.heading != blank -%}
          <h2 class="casa-sz__h2">{{ s.heading }}</h2>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if s.subheading != blank -%}
          <p class="casa-sz__lede">{{ s.subheading }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      <div class="casa-sz__copy">
        <div class="casa-sz__detail">
          {%- comment -%}
            The column reads as three groups, not eleven stacked lines:
            SPEC (what you picked) -> CHOOSE (pick another) -> BUY.
            Everything inside a group is 8-16px apart, groups are 40px
            apart, so the eye chunks it instead of scanning a list.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="casa-sz__spec">
          <div class="casa-sz__detail-head">
            <span class="casa-sz__detail-name" data-casa-sz-name>{{ init_name }}</span>
            <span class="casa-sz__pop" data-casa-sz-pop{% unless init_pop %} hidden{% endunless %}>BESTSELLER</span>
          </div>
          {%- comment -%}
            HEADLINE — inches, primary.
            For non-circle: W × H × D in inches.
            For circle: Ø × D in inches (since width = height = diameter).
            All depth values render the trailing inch symbol as literal
            HTML after the span (NOT as part of the value), because
            embedding " in a data-* attribute breaks HTML parsing.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The unit mark MUST live inside a `.nu` wrapper with its number.
            This row is a flex container with a 6px gap, and a bare `"` text
            node becomes its own anonymous flex item — so the mark was given
            the full gap and floated away from the figure it belongs to
            (`11.8 " × 11.8 " × 5 "`). Wrapping makes number+mark a single
            flex item with normal inline flow inside it.

            A dimension triple shares one unit, so only the last figure
            carries the mark; ″ is the true prime, not a straight quote.
            The circle branch keeps a mark on both figures because Ø and
            depth are two separate measurements, not a triple.

            Each data-casa-sz-* attribute must stay UNIQUE — the JS reads
            them with querySelector, so a duplicated hook would silently
            stop updating on size change.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            THREE BRANCHES, not two, because H = W is only true for some of
            these shapes.

            A regular octagon, a square and a circle all have a square
            bounding box, so printing the one published figure twice is
            accurate. A regular hexagon and an equilateral triangle do not:
            their height is width x 0.866. Printing "27.6 x 27.6" there
            states a height no source supports — the variant names and the
            product description both publish ONE figure per size
            (11.8" / 30cm, 19.7" / 50cm, 27.6" / 70cm), there is no
            dimension metafield, and the second number was coming from this
            template reusing the first.

            So non-square shapes show the published figure and the depth,
            and nothing that was not measured. Interim: once Coco confirms
            whether that figure is the width or the height, this branch can
            carry the real W x H.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="casa-sz__big" data-casa-sz-shape="{{ mirror_shape }}">
            {%- if is_circle -%}
              <span class="phi" aria-hidden="true">Ø</span>
              <span class="nu"><span data-casa-sz-inches>{{ init_in }}</span><span class="u" aria-hidden="true">″</span></span>
              <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
              <span class="nu"><span data-casa-sz-d-in>{{ init_d_in }}</span><span class="u" aria-hidden="true">″</span></span>
              <span class="lab-in" aria-hidden="true">deep</span>
            {%- elsif mirror_aspect != '1' -%}
              <span class="nu"><span data-casa-sz-inches>{{ init_in }}</span><span class="u" aria-hidden="true">″</span></span>
              <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
              <span class="nu"><span data-casa-sz-d-in>{{ init_d_in }}</span><span class="u" aria-hidden="true">″</span></span>
              <span class="lab-in" aria-hidden="true">deep</span>
            {%- else -%}
              <span data-casa-sz-inches>{{ init_in }}</span>
              <span class="x" aria-hidden="true">×</span>
              <span data-casa-sz-h-in>{{ init_in }}</span>
              <span class="x" aria-hidden="true">×</span>
              <span class="nu"><span data-casa-sz-d-in>{{ init_d_in }}</span><span class="u" aria-hidden="true">″</span></span>
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              The × and ″ are aria-hidden, so without this a screen reader
              announces a bare "27.6 27.6 5" with no unit at all.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if is_circle -%}
              <span class="visually-hidden">inches: diameter, then depth</span>
            {%- elsif mirror_aspect != '1' -%}
              <span class="visually-hidden">inches: size across, then depth</span>
            {%- else -%}
              <span class="visually-hidden">inches: width, height, depth</span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
          {%- comment -%}
            cm row and room-suggestion are one caption block. They used to
            be two separate elements with their own margins, which read as
            two more competing lines under the headline.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="casa-sz__specmeta">
          <div class="casa-sz__metric-row" aria-label="Dimensions in centimeters">
            {%- comment -%}
              No axis key. W x H x D is the standard order for decor
              dimensions, and every size in this family is square — W and H
              are literally the same number — so the key was labelling an
              order the reader cannot misread. The circle branch is
              self-describing for the same reason: the values carry "Ø" and
              "deep" inline.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if is_circle -%}
              <span class="vals">
                Ø <span data-casa-sz-cm>{{ init_cm }}</span> cm ·
                <span data-casa-sz-d-cm>{{ init_d_cm }}</span> deep
              </span>
            {%- elsif mirror_aspect != '1' -%}
              <span class="vals">
                <span data-casa-sz-cm>{{ init_cm }}</span> cm ·
                <span data-casa-sz-d-cm>{{ init_d_cm }}</span> deep
              </span>
            {%- else -%}
              <span class="vals">
                <span data-casa-sz-cm>{{ init_cm }}</span> ×
                <span data-casa-sz-h-cm>{{ init_cm }}</span> ×
                <span data-casa-sz-d-cm>{{ init_d_cm }}</span>
              </span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
          <div class="casa-sz__use" data-casa-sz-use>{{ init_use }}</div>
          </div>{%- comment -%} /specmeta {%- endcomment -%}
          </div>{%- comment -%} /spec {%- endcomment -%}

          <div class="casa-sz__choose">
          <div class="casa-sz__pick">
            <button class="casa-sz__chip" data-casa-sz-chip="S" type="button"
              data-h-in="{{ hin_s }}" data-h-cm="{{ hcm_s }}"
              data-depth-in="{{ depth_s_in }}" data-depth-cm="{{ depth_s_cm }}"
              {% if v_s %} data-variant-id="{{ v_s.id }}" data-variant-available="{{ v_s.available }}" data-variant-url="{{ v_s.url }}" data-variant-option="{{ v_s.option1 | escape }}" data-use="{{ use_s | escape }}" data-price="{{ p_s | escape }}" data-was="{{ w_s | escape }}"{% endif %}>
              <span class="k">Small</span>
              <span class="d">11.8"</span>
            </button>
            <button class="casa-sz__chip" data-casa-sz-chip="M" type="button"
              data-h-in="{{ hin_m }}" data-h-cm="{{ hcm_m }}"
              data-depth-in="{{ depth_m_in }}" data-depth-cm="{{ depth_m_cm }}"
              {% if v_m %} data-variant-id="{{ v_m.id }}" data-variant-available="{{ v_m.available }}" data-variant-url="{{ v_m.url }}" data-variant-option="{{ v_m.option1 | escape }}" data-use="{{ use_m | escape }}" data-price="{{ p_m | escape }}" data-was="{{ w_m | escape }}"{% endif %}>
              <span class="k">Medium</span>
              <span class="d">19.7"</span>
            </button>
            <button class="casa-sz__chip" data-casa-sz-chip="L" type="button"
              data-h-in="{{ hin_l }}" data-h-cm="{{ hcm_l }}"
              data-depth-in="{{ depth_l_in }}" data-depth-cm="{{ depth_l_cm }}"
              {% if v_l %} data-variant-id="{{ v_l.id }}" data-variant-available="{{ v_l.available }}" data-variant-url="{{ v_l.url }}" data-variant-option="{{ v_l.option1 | escape }}" data-use="{{ use_l | escape }}" data-price="{{ p_l | escape }}" data-was="{{ w_l | escape }}"{% endif %}>
              <span class="k">Large</span>
              <span class="d">27.6"</span>
              <span class="pop">POPULAR</span>
            </button>
          </div>


          {%- if show_atc -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            BUY — the third group the column was designed around.

            Progressive enhancement, same contract as casa-sizing-cube:
            the element is a real <a> pointing at the selected variant's
            URL, so with JS off (or if the fetch throws) the click still
            takes the shopper to a correctly-selected PDP. The script
            intercepts it and adds via cart/add.js instead.

            aria-live on the label, not the button: the button owns a
            name that must stay stable for AT, while the label swaps
            through Adding… / Added. Announcing the region announces the
            state change without renaming the control.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="casa-sz__buy">
            <div class="casa-sz__price-row">
              <span class="casa-sz__price" data-casa-sz-price>{{ p_def }}</span>
              {%- comment -%}
                <s> is the element, not a line-through class: the
                strikethrough is semantic here (this price no longer
                applies) and must survive a stylesheet that fails to
                load. Hidden rather than absent so the JS can fill it
                when the shopper moves to a size that IS on offer.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <s class="casa-sz__was" data-casa-sz-was{% if w_def == blank %} hidden{% endif %}>{{ w_def }}</s>
            </div>
            <a
              class="casa-sz__atc btn btn-primary"
              href="{{ v_def.url }}"
              data-casa-sz-atc="{{ v_def.id }}"
              {% unless v_def.available %}aria-disabled="true" data-soldout="1"{% endunless %}
            >
              <span class="casa-sz__atc-label" aria-live="polite">{% if v_def.available %}Add to cart{% else %}Sold out{% endif %}</span>
            </a>
          </div>
          {%- endif -%}

          </div>{%- comment -%} /choose {%- endcomment -%}
        </div>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%} RIGHT — true-scale plate {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="casa-sz__scene-wrap">
        <div class="casa-sz__plate{% if plate_mode == 'photo' %} casa-sz__plate--photo{% endif %}" data-casa-sz-plate>
          {%- comment -%}
            Plate is 80" wide × 40" tall.
            S/M/L positioned at centerlines 13"/36"/64" from left.
            Sizes drawn at exact relative scale: 11.8 / 19.7 / 27.6".

            The three mirrors live inside .casa-sz__field, an inner band
            inset a FIXED number of px from the plate edges (see CSS). That
            inset guarantees the outermost annotations — the Large mirror's
            right-hand height arrow and the Small mirror's centered size tag
            — always have room and are never sliced by the plate's
            overflow:hidden, even at 280px. A px inset (not %) is deliberate:
            it stays constant across viewports, so it's a big-enough margin
            on a narrow phone plate without wasting space on a wide desktop
            plate. Mirror left/width % now resolve against the field, so the
            three stay at exact relative scale.
          {%- endcomment -%}

          {%- if plate_mode == 'photo' -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            PHOTO SCENE — the real room, with only the selected mirror lit.

            Two photographs of the SAME scene at the same framing: one with
            all three mirrors off, one with all three on. The off frame is
            the base, darkened and tinted so it reads as the same night
            room; the on frame sits above it, revealed only through a soft
            radial mask over the selected mirror. So the lit mirror and the
            two dark ones are both real photography — nothing is faked or
            drawn, and the seam disappears into the mask's feather.

            The hotspots are NOT left-to-right by size. In this scene the
            order on the wall is Small, LARGE, Medium — getting that wrong
            lights the wrong mirror. Positions are settings because they
            are specific to this photograph; another product's scene will
            need its own.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign on_src = s.scene_on_image | image_url: width: 1400
            assign off_alt = s.scene_off_image.alt | default: product.title
          -%}
          <div class="casa-sz__scene">
            {{
              s.scene_off_image
              | image_url: width: 1400
              | image_tag:
                widths: '400, 600, 900, 1200, 1400',
                sizes: '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 620px',
                loading: 'lazy',
                decoding: 'async',
                class: 'casa-sz__scene-base',
                alt: off_alt
            }}
            <span class="casa-sz__scene-tint" aria-hidden="true"></span>

            <span class="casa-sz__lit" data-casa-sz-lit="S" aria-hidden="true"
              style="background-image:url({{ on_src }});--mx:{{ s.hot_s_x }}%;--my:{{ s.hot_s_y }}%;--mr:{{ s.hot_s_r }}%;"></span>
            <span class="casa-sz__lit" data-casa-sz-lit="M" aria-hidden="true"
              style="background-image:url({{ on_src }});--mx:{{ s.hot_m_x }}%;--my:{{ s.hot_m_y }}%;--mr:{{ s.hot_m_r }}%;"></span>
            <span class="casa-sz__lit" data-casa-sz-lit="L" aria-hidden="true"
              style="background-image:url({{ on_src }});--mx:{{ s.hot_l_x }}%;--my:{{ s.hot_l_y }}%;--mr:{{ s.hot_l_r }}%;"></span>

            <button type="button" class="casa-sz__hot" data-casa-sz-hot="S"
              aria-label="Small — {{ hin_s }} inches"
              style="left:{{ s.hot_s_x }}%;top:{{ s.hot_s_y }}%;width:{{ s.hot_s_w }}%;"></button>
            <button type="button" class="casa-sz__hot" data-casa-sz-hot="M"
              aria-label="Medium — {{ hin_m }} inches"
              style="left:{{ s.hot_m_x }}%;top:{{ s.hot_m_y }}%;width:{{ s.hot_m_w }}%;"></button>
            <button type="button" class="casa-sz__hot" data-casa-sz-hot="L"
              aria-label="Large — {{ hin_l }} inches"
              style="left:{{ s.hot_l_x }}%;top:{{ s.hot_l_y }}%;width:{{ s.hot_l_w }}%;"></button>
          </div>
          {%- else -%}
          <div class="casa-sz__field">
          {%- comment -%} SMALL — 11.8" {%- endcomment -%}
          <button class="casa-sz__mirror" data-casa-sz-mirror="S" type="button"
            aria-label="Small — 11.8 inches"
            style="left:16.25%;top:50%;width:14.75%;aspect-ratio:{{ mirror_aspect }};transform:translate(-50%,-50%);">
            <svg viewBox="{{ svg_viewbox }}" preserveAspectRatio="none" class="casa-sz__mirror-svg">
              {%- comment -%} glass: the tunnel void behind the reflections {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- if is_circle -%}
                <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="49" class="casa-sz__glass" />
              {%- else -%}
                <polygon points="{{ poly_pts }}" class="casa-sz__glass" />
              {%- endif -%}
              {{ tunnel_svg }}
              {%- comment -%} frame, drawn last so it caps the reflections {%- endcomment-%}
              {%- if is_circle -%}
                <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="49"
                  class="casa-sz__poly" data-casa-sz-poly="S"
                  stroke-width="1.2" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
              {%- else -%}
                <polygon points="{{ poly_pts }}"
                  class="casa-sz__poly" data-casa-sz-poly="S"
                  stroke-width="1.2" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
              {%- endif -%}
            </svg>
          </button>

          {%- comment -%} MEDIUM — 19.7" {%- endcomment -%}
          <button class="casa-sz__mirror" data-casa-sz-mirror="M" type="button"
            aria-label="Medium — 19.7 inches"
            style="left:45%;top:50%;width:24.625%;aspect-ratio:{{ mirror_aspect }};transform:translate(-50%,-50%);">
            <svg viewBox="{{ svg_viewbox }}" preserveAspectRatio="none" class="casa-sz__mirror-svg">
              {%- comment -%} glass: the tunnel void behind the reflections {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- if is_circle -%}
                <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="49" class="casa-sz__glass" />
              {%- else -%}
                <polygon points="{{ poly_pts }}" class="casa-sz__glass" />
              {%- endif -%}
              {{ tunnel_svg }}
              {%- comment -%} frame, drawn last so it caps the reflections {%- endcomment-%}
              {%- if is_circle -%}
                <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="49"
                  class="casa-sz__poly" data-casa-sz-poly="M"
                  stroke-width="1.2" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
              {%- else -%}
                <polygon points="{{ poly_pts }}"
                  class="casa-sz__poly" data-casa-sz-poly="M"
                  stroke-width="1.2" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
              {%- endif -%}
            </svg>
          </button>

          {%- comment -%} LARGE — 27.6" {%- endcomment -%}
          <button class="casa-sz__mirror" data-casa-sz-mirror="L" type="button"
            aria-label="Large — 27.6 inches"
            style="left:80%;top:50%;width:34.5%;aspect-ratio:{{ mirror_aspect }};transform:translate(-50%,-50%);">
            <svg viewBox="{{ svg_viewbox }}" preserveAspectRatio="none" class="casa-sz__mirror-svg">
              {%- comment -%} glass: the tunnel void behind the reflections {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- if is_circle -%}
                <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="49" class="casa-sz__glass" />
              {%- else -%}
                <polygon points="{{ poly_pts }}" class="casa-sz__glass" />
              {%- endif -%}
              {{ tunnel_svg }}
              {%- comment -%} frame, drawn last so it caps the reflections {%- endcomment-%}
              {%- if is_circle -%}
                <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="49"
                  class="casa-sz__poly" data-casa-sz-poly="L"
                  stroke-width="1.2" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
              {%- else -%}
                <polygon points="{{ poly_pts }}"
                  class="casa-sz__poly" data-casa-sz-poly="L"
                  stroke-width="1.2" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
              {%- endif -%}
            </svg>
          </button>

          {%- comment -%}
            DIMENSION BRACKET — one hairline down the selected mirror's left
            flank, one across its bottom, meeting at the corner, each figure
            riding its own rail.

            It lives inside the field so the field is its offsetParent and
            the JS can position it straight off the selected mirror's offset
            box. Geometry is set from JS in px, not Liquid in %, because the
            plate's aspect-ratio changes at <=640px and the mirror's own
            aspect-ratio changes per shape — a % scheme would be wrong on
            both axes.

            Decorative: every mirror button already carries its own
            aria-label ("Small - 11.8 inches"), and the copy column states
            the full W x H x D triple with its own visually-hidden axis key.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="casa-sz__bracket" data-casa-sz-bracket aria-hidden="true">
            {%- comment -%}
              The height rail ships only where the drawing is SQUARE.

              hin_* carries the frame's published height, which equals its
              width for every mirror in this lineup (27.5" x 27.5"). But
              hexagon-flat and triangle draw at mirror_aspect 1.1547, so the
              artwork is 86.6% as tall as it is wide. Measured at a 620px
              plate with Large selected: the box is 213.2 x 184.6, so a
              height rail would span 142px against the width rail's 168px
              while both read 27.6" — a drawing contradicting its own
              labels, on the live HexaMirror and TriMirror PDPs.

              Suppressing the rail is the honest read of today's geometry,
              not the fix. The fix is to letterbox those two polygons inside
              a square viewBox so the artwork matches the published spec —
              which also touches casa-day-night-transform and
              casa-install-steps, and is the follow-up 625fc44f already
              flagged. Width and depth are unaffected and still render.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if mirror_aspect == '1' -%}
              <span class="casa-sz__rail casa-sz__rail--h casa-sz__rail--h-a"></span>
              <span class="casa-sz__rail casa-sz__rail--h casa-sz__rail--h-b"></span>
              <span class="casa-sz__serif casa-sz__serif--v casa-sz__serif--v-top"></span>
              <span class="casa-sz__serif casa-sz__serif--v casa-sz__serif--v-bot"></span>
              <span class="casa-sz__dim casa-sz__dim--v" data-casa-sz-bk-h>{{ hin_l }}&Prime;</span>
            {%- endif -%}
            <span class="casa-sz__rail casa-sz__rail--w casa-sz__rail--w-a"></span>
            <span class="casa-sz__rail casa-sz__rail--w casa-sz__rail--w-b"></span>
            <span class="casa-sz__serif casa-sz__serif--h casa-sz__serif--h-l"></span>
            <span class="casa-sz__serif casa-sz__serif--h casa-sz__serif--h-r"></span>
            <span class="casa-sz__dim casa-sz__dim--w" data-casa-sz-bk-w>{{ hin_l }}&Prime;</span>
          </div>
          </div>

          {%- comment -%}
            DEPTH LEGEND — deliberately NOT on the shape. The plate is a
            true-scale elevation, so the third axis points at the viewer and
            has nothing to measure against. It sits in the one corner no
            selection reaches: Small is the leftmost mirror and it is
            vertically centred, so the top-left is dead plate at every size.

            The mark is two hairlines — a wall, and how far the mirror
            stands off it. Enough to say "third axis" without drawing a box.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="casa-sz__depth" data-casa-sz-depth-box aria-hidden="true">
            <svg class="casa-sz__depth-mark" viewBox="0 0 14 12" width="14" height="12"
              fill="none" stroke-width="1" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              {%- comment -%}
                Stroked from CSS, not a stroke="" attribute, so the wall and
                the measure stay tied to the same --bk-hair / --bk-lab tokens
                the rails and figures use. A presentation attribute would
                drift the moment either value changed.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <path class="casa-sz__depth-wall" d="M1 0V12" />
              <path class="casa-sz__depth-span" d="M1 6H12M12 3V9" />
            </svg>
            <span class="casa-sz__depth-txt">
              <span class="casa-sz__depth-k">Depth</span>
              <span class="casa-sz__depth-v"><span data-casa-sz-depth-plate>{{ depth_l_in }}</span>&Prime;</span>
            </span>
          </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>

        {%- if s.room_image != blank -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The photograph does what no SVG can: it makes the size
            believable, and it restores the three-way comparison the
            photo scene gives up by lighting one mirror at a time.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <figure class="casa-sz__room">
            {{
              s.room_image
              | image_url: width: 1400
              | image_tag:
                widths: '400, 600, 900, 1200, 1400',
                sizes: '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60vw',
                loading: 'lazy',
                decoding: 'async',
                alt: s.room_image.alt | default: product.title
            }}
            {%- if s.room_caption != blank -%}
              <figcaption>{{ s.room_caption | escape }}</figcaption>
            {%- endif -%}
          </figure>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{% stylesheet %}
.casa-sz {
  background: var(--casa-sz-paper, var(--casa-paper));
  padding: var(--casa-sz-pt, 96px) 0 var(--casa-sz-pb, 96px);
  color: var(--casa-sz-ink, var(--casa-ink));
}
.casa-sz__cap {
  max-width: 1440px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 0 clamp(20px, 5vw, 64px);
}
.casa-sz__mono {
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
  font-size: 11px;
  letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--casa-sz-ink);
  margin: 0;
}
.casa-sz__mono--dim { color: #8A8A92; }

/* The mirrors are the point of this section, so the plate gets the room
   and the copy column narrows. Areas (not source order) drive the layout,
   which is what lets the phone put the plate directly under the heading
   while desktop keeps heading and spec stacked in the left column. */
.casa-sz__inner {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(240px, 330px) 1fr;
  grid-template-areas:
    "intro scene"
    "copy  scene";
  gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 64px);
  align-content: center;
}
/* The plate spans both rows, so it sets their heights and the two left
   items would otherwise sit at the top of a much taller column with dead
   space under the chips. Pushing intro to the bottom of its row and copy
   to the top of the next makes the pair meet in the middle — vertically
   centred against the plate without needing them in one wrapper (which
   would cost the mobile reorder). */
.casa-sz__intro { grid-area: intro; min-width: 0; align-self: end; }
.casa-sz__copy  { grid-area: copy;  min-width: 0; align-self: start; }
.casa-sz__scene-wrap { grid-area: scene; }
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .casa-sz__inner {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas:
      "intro"
      "scene"
      "copy";
    gap: 28px;
  }
  /* single column — rows are content-height, so nothing to pull together */
  .casa-sz__intro,
  .casa-sz__copy { align-self: stretch; }
}

.casa-sz__copy {
  min-width: 0;
  /* One scale for the whole column. Before this there were eight
     different gap values down these eleven elements and no grouping, so
     every line read as its own island — that, not the amount of content,
     is what made it feel cluttered. */
  --sz-1: 8px;
  --sz-2: 16px;
  --sz-3: 24px;
  --sz-4: 40px;
}
.casa-sz__h2 {
  font-family: var(--casa-pdp-h2-family);
  font-size: var(--casa-pdp-h2-size);
  font-weight: var(--casa-pdp-h2-weight);
  letter-spacing: var(--casa-pdp-h2-tracking);
  line-height: var(--casa-pdp-h2-line);
  margin: 18px 0 0;
  text-wrap: balance;
}
.casa-sz__h2 em {
  /* Brand pattern: one <em> per heading. display:block forces it onto a new
     line, replacing the <br> that was previously in the default value (now
     forbidden because the field is inline_richtext, which only allows
     <em>/<strong>/<i>/<b>/<u>/<sup>/<sub>/<a>). */
  display: block;
  font-style: italic;
  font-weight: 800;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--casa-sz-purple), var(--casa-sz-pink));
  -webkit-background-clip: text;
  background-clip: text;
  -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
  color: transparent;
}
.casa-sz__lede {
  margin: var(--sz-2) 0 var(--sz-4);
  font-size: clamp(14px, 1.2vw, 16px);
  line-height: 1.55;
  color: #54545C;
  max-width: 40ch;
}

.casa-sz__detail {
  border-top: 1px solid #E1E1DC;
  padding-top: var(--sz-3);
}
/* group 1 -> group 2. The only separation that needs to be felt. */
.casa-sz__choose { margin-top: var(--sz-4); }
.casa-sz__detail-head {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 10px;
  margin-bottom: var(--sz-1);
}
.casa-sz__detail-name {
  font-family: var(--font-body--family);
  font-weight: 500;
  font-size: 14px;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--casa-sz-ink);
}
.casa-sz__pop {
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
  font-size: 9px;
  letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  background: var(--casa-sz-purple);
  color: #fff;
  padding: 3px 7px;
  border-radius: 100px;
}
.casa-sz__pop[hidden] { display: none; }

.casa-sz__big {
  font-family: var(--font-body--family);
  /* Fluid sizing tuned so the headline ALWAYS fits on one line at any
     viewport — clamp scales smoothly between mobile (~22px) and desktop
     (~36px). Pair with white-space: nowrap to forbid line breaks even
     when the container is narrower than expected. */
  font-size: clamp(22px, 2.8vw, 36px);
  font-weight: 300;
  letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  line-height: 1.05;
  margin: 0 0 var(--sz-2);
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: 6px;
  flex-wrap: nowrap;
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow-wrap: normal;
}
.casa-sz__big > * { flex-shrink: 0; }
/* Number + unit mark as ONE flex item, so the row's 6px gap can never open
   between a figure and its own ″. Inline-flex keeps them on a shared
   baseline while the mark itself is raised. */
.casa-sz__big .nu {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: baseline;
}
.casa-sz__big .u {
  font-size: 0.46em;
  font-weight: 400;
  color: #9A9AA2;
  letter-spacing: 0;
  /* A prime mark sits at cap height, not on the baseline. translateY pays
     nothing in layout, so the raise cannot reflow the row. */
  transform: translateY(-0.62em);
  margin-left: 0.06em;
}
.casa-sz__big .x {
  font-weight: 200;
  color: #9A9AA2;
  font-size: 0.78em;
  margin: 0 -2px;
}
.casa-sz__big .phi {
  font-weight: 300;
  color: #9A9AA2;
  font-size: 0.92em;
  margin-right: -2px;
}
.casa-sz__big .dot {
  font-weight: 300;
  color: #9A9AA2;
  font-size: 0.78em;
  margin: 0 -2px;
}
.casa-sz__big .lab-in {
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
  font-size: 0.32em;
  font-weight: 500;
  color: #9A9AA2;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.16em;
  margin-left: 4px;
  align-self: center;
}
.casa-sz__big .cm {
  font-size: 0.4em;
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
  font-weight: 400;
  color: #54545C;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  margin-left: 6px;
}
/* the cm row and the room suggestion, reading as one quiet two-line unit
   under the headline rather than two more competing rows. NOT named
   __caption — that class already belongs to the plate's "drawn to scale"
   line further down this stylesheet. */
.casa-sz__specmeta {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 6px;
  padding-left: 12px;
  border-left: 2px solid #E1E1DC;
}
.casa-sz__metric-row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: 10px;
  margin: 0;
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
  font-size: 12px;
  color: #54545C;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
.casa-sz__metric-row .vals {
  color: var(--casa-sz-ink, var(--casa-ink));
  font-weight: 500;
}
.casa-sz__use {
  font-size: 13px;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: #54545C;
  margin: 0;
}

.casa-sz__pick {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--sz-1);
}
.casa-sz__chip {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 4px;
  padding: 12px;
  background: #F2F2EE;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  border-radius: 8px;
  font-family: var(--font-body--family);
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background 0.18s, border-color 0.18s;
}
.casa-sz__chip:hover { background: #E8E8E4; }
.casa-sz__chip.is-on {
  background: var(--casa-sz-paper);
  border-color: var(--casa-sz-ink);
}
.casa-sz__chip .k {
  font-size: 11px;
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--casa-sz-ink);
}
.casa-sz__chip .d {
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
  font-size: 13px;
  color: #54545C;
}
.casa-sz__chip .pop {
  position: absolute;
  top: -7px;
  right: 8px;
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
  font-size: 8px;
  letter-spacing: 0.12em;
  background: var(--casa-sz-purple);
  color: #fff;
  padding: 2px 6px;
  border-radius: 100px;
}

/* ── BUY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Sits inside .casa-sz__choose, 16px under the chips — the same
   inside-a-group step the rest of the column uses. It is deliberately
   NOT the 40px between-group step: the price is a direct consequence of
   which chip is lit, and pushing it a group away orphans it from the
   thing that sets it.

   .casa-sz__choose is block layout with only a margin-top, so this
   margin-top is the sole spacing between the chips and this row — no
   stacked gap/margin pair to double it. */
/* A bare [hidden] loses here: the UA rule and `.casa-sz__buy` are both
   specificity 0,1,0 and this stylesheet ships after the UA sheet, so
   display:flex would win and `buyEl.hidden = true` would do nothing. */
.casa-sz__buy[hidden] { display: none; }
.casa-sz__buy {
  margin-top: var(--sz-2);
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 10px;
}
.casa-sz__price-row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  /* wrap, because a weak-unit currency is a long string: the same row
     that reads "$349.00 USD" reads "38.343.000 VND" in a Vietnamese
     market, and with a compare-at beside it that is two long strings on
     one line at 280px. Wrapping is the fix; nowrap here would push the
     was-price off the right edge. */
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 4px 10px;
  font-family: var(--font-body--family);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  font-feature-settings: 'tnum';
  min-width: 0;
}
.casa-sz__price {
  font-size: 20px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--casa-sz-ink);
  letter-spacing: -0.01em;
}
.casa-sz__was {
  font-size: 14px;
  /* #6B6B73 on #FAFAF7 is 5.1:1 — the muted #8A8A93 this started as
     measured 3.2:1 and fails AA for text this size. */
  color: #6B6B73;
}
.casa-sz__was[hidden] { display: none; }

.casa-sz__atc {
  width: 100%;
  /* .btn is inline-flex, so width:100% alone still shrink-wraps inside a
     flex column; the flex parent stretches it, but this keeps it full
     width if the row is ever re-parented into a block context. */
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
/* Sold out. The element stays an <a> rather than becoming a <span>
   because the shopper can move back to an in-stock size and the row has
   to recover in place — swapping tag names mid-interaction would drop
   focus. The click is refused in JS; this is the visual half. */
.casa-sz__atc[data-soldout] {
  background: #E1E1DC;
  color: #6B6B73;
  cursor: not-allowed;
}
.casa-sz__atc[data-soldout]:hover { transform: none; }
.casa-sz__atc[aria-busy='true'] { opacity: 0.75; }

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .casa-sz__atc { transition: none; }
  .casa-sz__atc:hover { transform: none; }
}

/* Plate */
.casa-sz__scene-wrap {
  min-width: 0;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 18px;
}
.casa-sz__plate {
  position: relative;
  aspect-ratio: 80 / 40;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, #FAFAF7 0%, #F2F2EE 100%);
  border: 1px solid #E1E1DC;
  border-radius: 12px;
  overflow: hidden;
}
/* In photo mode the scene brings its own square frame, so the plate has
   to stop being a fixed-ratio box — its 80/40 plus overflow:hidden was
   slicing the bottom off the photograph. */
.casa-sz__plate--photo {
  aspect-ratio: auto;
  background: none;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  overflow: visible;
}
/* Inner band the three mirrors are positioned within. Inset a FIXED px
   amount from the plate edges so the Large mirror's right-hand height
   arrow and the Small mirror's centered size tag always clear the plate's
   overflow:hidden clip — at any viewport (down to 280px), in any of the 5
   shapes. Fixed px (not %) keeps the margin adequate on a narrow phone
   plate without over-insetting the wide desktop plate (a % inset would
   waste desktop space). Mirror left/width % resolve against this band.

   Kept small (10px) so the mirrors stay close to full plate size on phones
   — a wider inset visibly shrank them. The one thing 10px can't clear on
   its own is the Small mirror's caption at ≤360px (it's centred under the
   smallest, left-most mirror and overhangs); the caption font-shrink under
   the ≤360px media query below covers that last few px. Together: near
   full-size mirrors AND nothing clipped down to 280px. */

/* ── PHOTO SCENE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Base is the lights-off frame, pushed down to night with brightness and
   desaturation plus a teal wash, so it matches the lit frame's colour of
   light. The lit frame is then revealed through a soft radial mask over
   one mirror. Both layers are real photographs of the same scene, so the
   only thing being faked is which lamp is switched on. */
.casa-sz__scene {
  position: relative;
  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
  max-width: 620px;
  margin-inline: auto;
  border-radius: 12px;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: #0C1416;
}
.casa-sz__scene-base {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  filter: brightness(0.55) saturate(0.6);
}
.casa-sz__scene-tint {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: rgb(10, 60, 62);
  opacity: 0.18;
  pointer-events: none;
}
.casa-sz__lit {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
  /* Ellipse, not circle: percentage radii are only allowed on ellipse.
     The scene is square so both radii resolve to the same length. */
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse var(--mr) var(--mr) at var(--mx) var(--my), #000 0 62%, transparent 100%);
  mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse var(--mr) var(--mr) at var(--mx) var(--my), #000 0 62%, transparent 100%);
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.55s ease;
  pointer-events: none;
}
.casa-sz__lit.is-on { opacity: 1; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .casa-sz__lit { transition: none; }
}
/* Click the mirror itself, not just the chip. Round because the mirrors
   are octagons — a square target would overlap its neighbour. */
.casa-sz__hot {
  position: absolute;
  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  background: none;
  border: 0;
  padding: 0;
  border-radius: 50%;
  cursor: pointer;
}
.casa-sz__hot:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid #fff;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* the room photograph under the plate */
.casa-sz__room { margin: 16px 0 0; }
.casa-sz__room img {
  width: 100%;
  /* Capped and cropped. At natural height this photo is taller than the
     interactive frame above it and takes over the section — the evidence
     outranking the thing it is evidence for. Biased upward so the three
     mirrors stay in the crop. */
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  height: auto;
  object-fit: cover;
  /* 50% 38% put the crop window too low and cut the top off the middle
     mirror — the hero of the proof shot, decapitated. A 16:9 window keeps
     56% of a square source; 14% down the remaining travel starts it just
     above the mirrors and still catches the console beneath them. */
  object-position: 50% 14%;
  display: block;
  border-radius: 12px;
}
.casa-sz__room figcaption {
  margin-top: 10px;
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
  font-size: 11px;
  letter-spacing: 0.12em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: #54545C;
  text-align: center;
}

.casa-sz__field {
  position: absolute;
  /* Was inset 10px so the Large mirror's height arrow and the Small
     mirror's size tag were never sliced by the plate's overflow:hidden.
     Both are gone, so the mirrors get that space back. */
  inset: 0;
}

.casa-sz__mirror {
  position: absolute;
  background: none;
  border: 0;
  padding: 0;
  cursor: pointer;
  /* aspect-ratio is set inline per-instance based on mirror_shape — see Liquid case at top */
  transition: filter 0.25s ease;
}
/* Light spills onto the plate the way it spills onto the wall in the
   product shots — three stacked shadows, tight hot core to wide cool halo. */
.casa-sz__mirror.is-on {
  filter:
    drop-shadow(0 0 8px rgba(94, 10, 2, 0.45))
    drop-shadow(0 10px 30px rgba(176, 195, 250, 0.40))
    drop-shadow(0 0 56px rgba(242, 255, 160, 0.22));
}
.casa-sz__mirror-svg {
  width: 100%; height: 100%;
  display: block;
  overflow: visible;
}
/* ── OFF: plain mirror. ON: the tunnel. ─────────────────────────────
   Three layers, painted back to front:
     .casa-sz__glass   the surface — Paper when off, near-black when lit
     .casa-sz__tunnel  the reflections (spokes + nested rings)
     .casa-sz__poly    the frame, drawn last so it caps everything

   Reference: the lit product photography. What reads as depth is the
   SPOKE from each frame vertex to the vanishing point — concentric
   rings on their own look like a target. The old treatment was one
   radial gradient with a white-hot centre, which had neither. */
.casa-sz__glass {
  fill: var(--casa-sz-paper);
  transition: fill 0.3s ease;
}
.casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__glass { fill: #08080C; }

.casa-sz__poly {
  fill: none;
  stroke: var(--casa-sz-ink);
  /* perf-justified: stroke-width is an SVG paint property, not CSS width —
     it repaints, it does not reflow. The linter substring-matches "width". */
  transition: stroke-width 0.25s ease;
}
.casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__poly { stroke-width: 2.4; }

.casa-sz__tunnel {
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.2s ease;
}
.casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__tunnel { opacity: 1; }
.casa-sz__ring { stroke-width: 1; }
.casa-sz__spoke { stroke-width: 0.9; stroke-opacity: 0.62; }

/* Depth opacity lives in --o so the chase keyframe can return to it. Set
   as a custom property, not a stroke-opacity attribute, because a CSS
   animation on stroke-opacity would otherwise clobber the per-ring falloff
   and flatten the tunnel. */
.casa-sz__ring,
.casa-sz__spoke { stroke-opacity: var(--o, 1); }

/* CONTINUOUS CHASE. A band of brightness travels from the frame down into
   the vanishing point, forever — which is what an RGB-IC mirror running a
   chase effect actually does. The positive per-ring stagger is what makes
   it travel INWARD; the first pass doubles as the ignite when a size is
   selected, because the rule only matches once .is-on lands. */
@keyframes casa-sz-chase {
  0%, 100% { stroke-opacity: var(--o, 1); }
  18%      { stroke-opacity: 1; }
}
/* RGB-IC never sits still. Slow, small, and only on the lit mirror.
   perf-justified: gated on .is-live, which an IntersectionObserver in this
   section's JS toggles off once the plate leaves the viewport (+120px), so
   it is not compositing a filter while the shopper is elsewhere on the
   page. Falls back to always-on where IntersectionObserver is missing. */
@keyframes casa-sz-drift {
  0%, 100% { filter: hue-rotate(0deg); }
  50%      { filter: hue-rotate(24deg); }
}
.casa-sz.is-live .casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__ring,
.casa-sz.is-live .casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__spoke {
  /* perf-justified: gated on .is-live, toggled off by an IntersectionObserver
     in this section's JS once the plate leaves the viewport (+120px). */
  animation: casa-sz-chase 2.8s ease-in-out infinite;
  animation-delay: calc(var(--i, 0) * 0.16s);
}
.casa-sz.is-live .casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__tunnel {
  /* perf-justified: same .is-live IntersectionObserver gate as above. */
  animation: casa-sz-drift 14s ease-in-out 600ms infinite;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .casa-sz.is-live .casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__ring,
  .casa-sz.is-live .casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__spoke,
  .casa-sz.is-live .casa-sz__mirror.is-on .casa-sz__tunnel { animation: none; }
}

/* At ≤360px the Small mirror's caption is the one thing the small 10px
   field inset can't keep off the plate's left edge (it's centred under the
   smallest, left-most mirror and overhangs). Shave ~1px off each caption
   line so it narrows enough to clear the clip — verified to leave the
   Small tag ≥+3px inside the plate at 280px — while the mirrors keep their
   near-full size. Secondary caption text only; readability is unaffected. */


/* ── DIMENSION BRACKET ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   Left flank and bottom, never the right. With Large selected the right
   gutter is 2.75% of the plate — 10px on a 350px phone plate — which is
   what sliced the old arrows and why a 10px field inset had to exist to
   hold them. Left is 5.45% and below is 15.5%, so the bracket fits inside
   the plate's own overflow:hidden and the inset stays deleted.

   --bk-l/t/w/h are written by the section's JS off the selected mirror's
   measured offset box. */
.casa-sz__bracket,
.casa-sz__depth {
  /* One hairline value and one figure value for the whole annotation layer —
     rails, serifs and the depth mark's two strokes. Held here rather than as
     literals so the CSS and the inline SVG cannot drift apart.
     --bk-hair is 1.92:1 on Paper, under WCAG 1.4.11's 3:1. Deliberate: these
     are decorative rules, not "graphics required to understand the content" —
     the figures carry every value, and the copy column states the full
     W x H x D triple as text with its own visually-hidden axis key. */
  --bk-hair: #B9B7B0;
  --bk-lab: #54545C;
}
.casa-sz__bracket {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  --bk-gap: 12px;
}
.casa-sz__bracket.is-compact { --bk-gap: 9px; }

.casa-sz__rail,
.casa-sz__serif { position: absolute; background: var(--bk-hair); }

/* Each rail is TWO segments with a gap where its figure sits, rather than one
   run with a painted chip behind the text. A chip cannot work here: the lit
   mirror throws a purple/cyan drop-shadow across the plate, so any flat or
   gradient fill matched to the plate reads as a pale rectangle sitting in the
   glow. --bk-cut-v/w are half the figure's own measured box plus breathing
   room, written by the JS. max(0px, …) because at the Small selection on a
   narrow plate the figure is taller than half its rail, and a negative height
   would drop the declaration rather than collapse the segment. */
.casa-sz__rail--h {
  width: 1px;
  left: calc(var(--bk-l, 0px) - var(--bk-gap));
  height: max(0px, calc(var(--bk-h, 0px) / 2 - var(--bk-cut-v, 0px)));
}
.casa-sz__rail--h-a { top: var(--bk-t, 0px); }
.casa-sz__rail--h-b {
  top: calc(var(--bk-t, 0px) + var(--bk-h, 0px) / 2 + var(--bk-cut-v, 0px));
}
.casa-sz__rail--w {
  height: 1px;
  width: max(0px, calc(var(--bk-w, 0px) / 2 - var(--bk-cut-w, 0px)));
  top: calc(var(--bk-t, 0px) + var(--bk-h, 0px) + var(--bk-gap));
}
.casa-sz__rail--w-a { left: var(--bk-l, 0px); }
.casa-sz__rail--w-b {
  left: calc(var(--bk-l, 0px) + var(--bk-w, 0px) / 2 + var(--bk-cut-w, 0px));
}
.casa-sz__serif--v {
  width: 5px;
  height: 1px;
  left: calc(var(--bk-l, 0px) - var(--bk-gap) - 2px);
}
.casa-sz__serif--v-top { top: var(--bk-t, 0px); }
.casa-sz__serif--v-bot { top: calc(var(--bk-t, 0px) + var(--bk-h, 0px)); }
.casa-sz__serif--h {
  width: 1px;
  height: 5px;
  top: calc(var(--bk-t, 0px) + var(--bk-h, 0px) + var(--bk-gap) - 2px);
}
.casa-sz__serif--h-l { left: var(--bk-l, 0px); }
.casa-sz__serif--h-r { left: calc(var(--bk-l, 0px) + var(--bk-w, 0px)); }

/* Both figures ride their rail rather than sitting beside it. Below the
   rail needs ~23px of clear plate; the Large selection has 19px there at a
   250px plate, which put the figure through the plate floor and onto the
   artwork. Centred on the rail it needs half that. */
.casa-sz__dim {
  position: absolute;
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
  font-size: 11px;
  /* Pinned, not `normal`. The rail's break is sized from this box, and no
     @font-face for JetBrains Mono ships in this theme — the stack always
     falls through to the platform mono (SF Mono / Roboto Mono / Consolas),
     whose `normal` line boxes differ by a couple of px. Left free, the
     clearance to the neighbouring mirror at the is-tiny boundary rides on a
     font we do not control. */
  line-height: 1;
  color: var(--bk-lab);
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
.casa-sz__bracket.is-compact .casa-sz__dim { font-size: 9px; }
.casa-sz__dim--v {
  left: calc(var(--bk-l, 0px) - var(--bk-gap));
  top: calc(var(--bk-t, 0px) + var(--bk-h, 0px) / 2);
  writing-mode: vertical-rl;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(180deg);
}
.casa-sz__dim--w {
  left: calc(var(--bk-l, 0px) + var(--bk-w, 0px) / 2);
  top: calc(var(--bk-t, 0px) + var(--bk-h, 0px) + var(--bk-gap));
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}

/* Set by the JS when the gutter to the left-hand neighbour is too tight to
   hold the height figure without crowding it — see placeBracket(). Not a
   width breakpoint: the gutter depends on which size is selected, and the
   figure's own box depends on the platform mono. */
.casa-sz__bracket.is-noheight .casa-sz__rail--h,
.casa-sz__bracket.is-noheight .casa-sz__serif--v,
.casa-sz__bracket.is-noheight .casa-sz__dim--v { display: none; }

/* ── DEPTH LEGEND ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Top-left is dead plate at every selection: Small is the leftmost mirror
   and it is vertically centred, so nothing ever reaches this corner. */
.casa-sz__depth {
  position: absolute;
  left: 18px;
  top: 18px;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 7px;
  pointer-events: none;
  font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
}
.casa-sz__depth-mark { display: block; flex: none; }
.casa-sz__depth-wall { stroke: var(--bk-hair); }
.casa-sz__depth-span { stroke: #8A8A92; }
.casa-sz__depth-txt { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1px; }
.casa-sz__depth-k {
  font-size: 9px;
  letter-spacing: 0.16em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  /* NOT the #8A8A92 the rest of this section's dim text uses: that is 3.28:1
     on Paper, and this renders at 9px (8px compact) — nowhere near WCAG's
     large-text exemption, so 1.4.3 wants the full 4.5:1. aria-hidden does not
     exempt visible text from contrast. 4.83:1, indistinguishable at 9px. */
  color: #6E6E76;
}
.casa-sz__depth-v {
  font-size: 11px;
  color: var(--bk-lab);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
.casa-sz__depth.is-compact { left: 12px; top: 12px; }
.casa-sz__depth.is-compact .casa-sz__depth-k { font-size: 8px; }
.casa-sz__depth.is-compact .casa-sz__depth-v { font-size: 9.5px; }
/* One row, not a stack: stacked it is 24px tall, and on a 210px plate that
   reaches down into the Small mirror. The word goes too — at 210px the
   legend ran to within 5.9px of the Medium mirror's corner, and "DEPTH" is
   ~30 of its ~62px. Dropping it pulls the box back to ~32px, clearing that
   corner by 28px. The mark carries the axis, and the copy column two
   columns over still says the word. */
.casa-sz__depth.is-tiny { left: 8px; top: 8px; }
.casa-sz__depth.is-tiny .casa-sz__depth-k { display: none; }
.casa-sz__depth.is-tiny .casa-sz__depth-txt {
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: 5px;
}

.casa-sz__caption {
  text-align: center;
}

@media (max-width: 640px) {
  /* Was 80/60 to leave vertical room under each mirror for its size tag.
     The tags are gone, so that height is now just empty plate — tightened
     so the mirrors fill more of it. */
  .casa-sz__plate { aspect-ratio: 80 / 46; }
  .casa-sz__plate--photo { aspect-ratio: auto; }
  .casa-sz__big { font-size: 36px; }
}

/* ── One-frame density ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   Opt-in via the "Section height" setting; the four sibling mirror PDPs
   on this section keep their current spacing.

   Desktop already fits once the section padding comes down — the scene
   sits beside the copy rather than under it, so nothing here needs to
   shrink. The cost is entirely on phones, where the layout stacks into
   intro → scene → copy. The scene is a square, so it takes the full
   column width in height too; capping it against the viewport is what
   buys the frame back.                                                 */
.casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__h2 { margin-top: 12px; }

/* The accent is display:block so it drops to its own line — right in the
   two-column layout, where the copy column is only ~260px and the heading
   has to break somewhere. Stacked on a phone the heading owns the full
   column: measured 317px needed against 350px available, so the break was
   costing a whole line at full brand size for no reason. Inline below the
   two-column breakpoint only. */
@media (max-width: 749px) {
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__h2 em { display: inline; }
}

/* Short viewports. Media already scales with vh; type and spacing did not,
   so on a 667px-tall phone the fixed costs alone overran the frame. These
   tiers key off HEIGHT, not width — the constraint here is how little
   vertical room a landscape phone or an older handset actually has. */



@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__inner { gap: 18px; }
  /* Square scene = width in height. Below the two-column breakpoint the
     column is the full viewport, so an uncapped square eats ~100% of the
     frame on its own. */
  /* Three caps, and the smallest wins. 100% lets the photo reach the full
     column instead of stopping at 87% of it; 46vh keeps it from doing that
     on a short phone, where a square at full width would eat the frame on
     its own; 620px is the source's own limit. On a 390x844 handset the
     column is the binding one, which is the intent — as large as the space
     honestly allows. */
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__scene { max-width: min(620px, 100%, 46vh); }
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__copy { --sz-1: 6px; --sz-2: 12px; --sz-3: 17px; --sz-4: 26px; }
}

/* Tablets: restore the two-column layout below the shared 900px
   breakpoint.

   The vh cap above is correct for a phone, where height is the scarce
   resource. On a tablet it is exactly wrong — measured at 870x934 it
   pinned the photo to 299px inside a 783px column, filling 38% of the
   space available to it. Height is not scarce there; width is plentiful.

   Rather than just relaxing the cap (which would fix the photo but push
   the section past a frame), this hands the band the layout it has room
   for: copy beside the photo instead of above it. The photo then takes
   its column width, and the section gets SHORTER because copy and image
   share vertical space.

   Compact-scoped, so the four sibling mirror PDPs keep the shared
   breakpoint. MUST stay after the block above — same specificity, so it
   wins on source order only. */
@media (min-width: 750px) and (max-width: 900px) {
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__inner {
    /* Narrower copy column than the desktop 240-330: the band has height
       to spare (measured 342px of frame headroom at 870x934) but not much
       width, so every pixel not spent on the copy goes to the photo. The
       widest thing the column must hold is the dimension headline, which
       measures ~200px here. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(210px, 260px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas:
      "intro scene"
      "copy  scene";
    gap: 24px;
    align-content: center;
  }
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__scene { max-width: min(620px, 100%); }
}

/* 790px, not 800: it has to catch a 768-tall landscape tablet, which
   the first attempt at 760 missed by eight pixels, without also firing
   on an 800-tall laptop that already fits and would only get smaller
   for nothing.

   Height tiers live LAST. They share specificity with the width
   queries above and only win on source order — placed earlier they
   silently lost, which is exactly what happened on the first pass. */
@media (max-height: 790px) {
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__scene { max-width: min(620px, 100%, 40vh); }
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__copy { --sz-1: 5px; --sz-2: 10px; --sz-3: 14px; --sz-4: 20px; }
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__inner { gap: 14px; }
}
@media (max-height: 640px) {
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__scene { max-width: min(620px, 100%, 34vh); }
  .casa-sz--compact .casa-sz__big { font-size: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 28px); }
}
{% endstylesheet %}

{% javascript %}
function casaSzInit(ROOT) {
  if (!ROOT || ROOT.dataset.casaSzInit === '1') return;
  ROOT.dataset.casaSzInit = '1';

  var $$ = function (sel) { return Array.prototype.slice.call(ROOT.querySelectorAll(sel)); };
  var $ = function (sel) { return ROOT.querySelector(sel); };

  var DATA = {
    S: { name: 'Small',  inches: 11.8, cm: 30, h_in: 11.8, h_cm: 30, depth_in: '1.6', depth_cm: '4 cm', use: 'Vanities, nooks, desks',             price: 249, was: 349, popular: false },
    M: { name: 'Medium', inches: 19.7, cm: 50, h_in: 19.7, h_cm: 50, depth_in: '2.0', depth_cm: '5 cm', use: 'Bedrooms, entryways, hallways',      price: 299, was: 399, popular: false },
    L: { name: 'Large',  inches: 27.6, cm: 70, h_in: 27.6, h_cm: 70, depth_in: '2.4', depth_cm: '6 cm', use: 'Above sofas, gaming rooms, lounges', popular: true  }
  };

  $$('[data-casa-sz-chip]').forEach(function (chip) {
    var k = chip.dataset.casaSzChip;
    if (!DATA[k]) return;
    if (chip.dataset.hIn)     DATA[k].h_in     = chip.dataset.hIn;
    if (chip.dataset.hCm)     DATA[k].h_cm     = chip.dataset.hCm;
    if (chip.dataset.depthIn) DATA[k].depth_in = chip.dataset.depthIn;
    if (chip.dataset.depthCm) DATA[k].depth_cm = chip.dataset.depthCm;
    if (chip.dataset.variantId) DATA[k].variant = chip.dataset.variantId;
    if (chip.dataset.variantAvailable) DATA[k].available = chip.dataset.variantAvailable !== 'false';
    if (chip.dataset.variantUrl) DATA[k].url = chip.dataset.variantUrl;
    /* Price strings are FORMATTED IN LIQUID and only carried here. Never
       build money in JS — the shopper's market decides the symbol, the
       decimal/thousands separators and whether a currency code shows,
       and none of that is knowable client-side. */
    if (chip.dataset.price) DATA[k].price_html = chip.dataset.price;
    DATA[k].was_html = chip.dataset.was || '';
    /* Liquid owns the copy; the table above is only the fallback. */
    if (chip.dataset.use) DATA[k].use = chip.dataset.use;
    /* The variant picker's radios are keyed by OPTION VALUE ("27.6\" / 70cm"),
       not by variant id — this is what lets a chip drive the picker. */
    if (chip.dataset.variantOption) DATA[k].optionValue = chip.dataset.variantOption;
  });

  var nameEl   = $('[data-casa-sz-name]');
  var inchesEl = $('[data-casa-sz-inches]');
  var hInEl    = $('[data-casa-sz-h-in]');
  var dInEl    = $('[data-casa-sz-d-in]');
  var cmEl     = $('[data-casa-sz-cm]');
  var hCmEl    = $('[data-casa-sz-h-cm]');
  var dCmEl    = $('[data-casa-sz-d-cm]');
  var useEl    = $('[data-casa-sz-use]');
  var popEl    = $('[data-casa-sz-pop]');
  var buyEl      = $('.casa-sz__buy');
  var priceEl    = $('[data-casa-sz-price]');
  var wasEl      = $('[data-casa-sz-was]');
  var atcEl      = $('[data-casa-sz-atc]');
  var atcLabelEl = atcEl ? atcEl.querySelector('.casa-sz__atc-label') : null;
  var mirrors  = $$('[data-casa-sz-mirror]');
  var chips    = $$('[data-casa-sz-chip]');
  var current  = ROOT.dataset.default || 'L';


  var lits = $$('[data-casa-sz-lit]');
  var hots = $$('[data-casa-sz-hot]');

  var fieldEl    = $('.casa-sz__field');
  var plateEl    = $('[data-casa-sz-plate]');
  var bracketEl  = $('[data-casa-sz-bracket]');
  var bkHEl      = $('[data-casa-sz-bk-h]');
  var bkWEl      = $('[data-casa-sz-bk-w]');
  var depthBoxEl = $('[data-casa-sz-depth-box]');
  var depthPlEl  = $('[data-casa-sz-depth-plate]');

  // The bracket is MEASURED, not computed. The plate's aspect-ratio changes
  // at <=640px and the mirror's own aspect-ratio changes per shape, so a
  // percentage scheme would be wrong on both axes. offsetLeft/offsetTop give
  // the mirror's CENTRE — each carries translate(-50%,-50%), which moves the
  // paint but not the offset box — so half the box comes back off each.
  // Absent in photo mode, where there is no field and no mirrors to measure.
  function placeBracket(k) {
    if (!bracketEl || !fieldEl) return;
    var m = fieldEl.querySelector('[data-casa-sz-mirror="' + k + '"]');
    if (!m) return;
    var w = m.offsetWidth;
    var h = m.offsetHeight;
    if (!w || !h) return;
    bracketEl.style.setProperty('--bk-l', (m.offsetLeft - w / 2) + 'px');
    bracketEl.style.setProperty('--bk-t', (m.offsetTop - h / 2) + 'px');
    bracketEl.style.setProperty('--bk-w', w + 'px');
    bracketEl.style.setProperty('--bk-h', h + 'px');

    // Tiers are keyed off the plate's real width, not a media query: the
    // plate is a column in a grid, so its width and the viewport's are not
    // the same number.
    var pw = plateEl ? plateEl.clientWidth : 0;
    var compact = pw > 0 && pw < 460;
    var tiny = pw > 0 && pw < 300;
    bracketEl.classList.toggle('is-compact', compact);
    bracketEl.classList.toggle('is-tiny', tiny);
    if (depthBoxEl) {
      depthBoxEl.classList.toggle('is-compact', compact);
      depthBoxEl.classList.toggle('is-tiny', tiny);
    }

    // The height figure lives in the gutter between this mirror and its left
    // neighbour, and that gutter is the tightest space on the plate: 5.45% of
    // the plate width with Large selected, which is 19px at a 350px phone
    // plate. Measured there, the figure cleared the Medium mirror by 5.1px —
    // technically not overlapping, visually touching.
    //
    // So the rule is the measurement, not a breakpoint: keep the figure only
    // where it clears the neighbour by MIN_CLEAR. That stays correct across
    // all 5 shapes, both plate aspect-ratios, and whichever platform mono the
    // stack falls through to — a hardcoded px width would be re-tuned by hand
    // every time one of those moved. Square frames mean height IS width, so
    // dropping it costs no fact and the copy column still states the triple.
    if (bkHEl) {
      var MIN_CLEAR = 10;
      // Un-hide BEFORE measuring. Measuring the figure while its own class
      // has it display:none reads offsetWidth 0, which shrinks `need` below
      // `available` and shows it again — then the next call measures the
      // visible box, hides it, and it flips back. The rule would oscillate
      // and settle on whatever the previous state happened to be.
      bracketEl.classList.remove('is-noheight');
      // Judge the TIGHTEST size on this plate, not the selected one. Per
      // selection, a 350px plate keeps the figure on Small and Medium and
      // drops it on Large — so tapping through the sizes makes a dimension
      // blink out, which reads as a bug rather than as a decision. One
      // verdict per plate: either this plate has room for a height figure or
      // it does not.
      var worstGutter = Infinity;
      mirrors.forEach(function (mirror) {
        var left = mirror.offsetLeft - mirror.offsetWidth / 2;
        var neighbourRight = 0;
        mirrors.forEach(function (other) {
          if (other === mirror) return;
          var oRight = other.offsetLeft + other.offsetWidth / 2;
          if (oRight <= left && oRight > neighbourRight) neighbourRight = oRight;
        });
        worstGutter = Math.min(worstGutter, left - neighbourRight);
      });
      var need = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(bracketEl).getPropertyValue('--bk-gap'))
        + bkHEl.offsetWidth / 2 + MIN_CLEAR;
      if (worstGutter < need) bracketEl.classList.add('is-noheight');
    }

    // Each rail breaks around its own figure, so it needs the figure's real
    // box — which depends on the font that actually loaded and on the tier
    // class just toggled above, not on anything computable in CSS. Read AFTER
    // the toggles so the measurement matches the size being rendered.
    // The vertical figure runs down its rail because of writing-mode, NOT the
    // rotate() — so its LENGTH is its offsetHeight. Drop the writing-mode and
    // keep the rotate and this reads the wrong axis.
    // bkHEl is absent by design on hexagon-flat and triangle, which draw at
    // aspect 1.1547 (see the markup comment on the height rail).
    if (bkHEl) {
      bracketEl.style.setProperty('--bk-cut-v', (bkHEl.offsetHeight / 2 + 5) + 'px');
    }
    if (bkWEl) {
      bracketEl.style.setProperty('--bk-cut-w', (bkWEl.offsetWidth / 2 + 6) + 'px');
    }
  }

  /* ── TWO-WAY SIZE SYNC ───────────────────────────────────────────────
     The strip, the buy box and the sticky bar are three surfaces showing
     one decision, so they have to agree.

     Picker -> strip is free: Horizon dispatches 'variant:update' on
     document with the variant at detail.resource (NOT detail.variant —
     casa-sticky-cart.js documents that trap, it froze the bar for a
     while). We just relight the matching chip.

     Strip -> picker is pushed IMMEDIATELY — never debounced. This used
     to wait 700ms so rapid comparison taps wouldn't each trigger the
     picker's full-page refetch (~1-2s, ~1MB — docs/variant-picker-
     architecture.md 6.6). That debounce was a money bug. The theme's
     variant system is a single-writer design with a lock: ~100ms after
     the picker learns of a change it DISABLES the buy buttons, and
     re-enables them only once the form's variant id is consistent
     again (measured 2026-08-24: disabled at 101ms, re-enabled with the
     new id at 1210ms). Wrong-item adds are impossible inside that lock.
     The debounce held the shopper's choice OUTSIDE it: for 700ms the
     picker knew nothing, no lock engaged, the buy buttons stayed live
     with the stale id — tap Large here, hit the sticky bar's ADD within
     that window, and the cart got Small (measured). Dispatching at once
     puts the tap on the picker's own guarded path instead.

     Rapid S -> M -> L taps are exactly what the picker's AbortController
     exists for — each new change cancels the in-flight fetch, so the
     last tap wins and the aborted transfers stop downloading.

     The strip itself never waits on any of this — syncBuy() has already
     repainted synchronously, so the chips feel instant and the rest of
     the page reconciles behind them. */
  function pushToPicker(k) {
    var d = DATA[k];
    if (!d || !d.variant || !d.optionValue) return;
    var picker = document.querySelector('variant-picker');
    if (!picker) return;
    var radios = picker.querySelectorAll('input[type="radio"]');
    for (var i = 0; i < radios.length; i++) {
      if (radios[i].value !== d.optionValue) continue;
      /* Skip only when the RADIO is already on this size. The form's
         hidden id is a TRAILING indicator (it updates ~1.2s later, after
         the fetch) — guarding on it here would skip a legitimate push
         mid-flight: S selected, tap L (fetch starts), tap S again — the
         form still says S, a form-guard skips, the L fetch lands, and
         the strip is stranded out of sync. The radio is the picker's
         intent and is always current. */
      if (radios[i].checked) return;
      /* Set state + fire 'change' — deliberately NOT .click().
         Clicking the label focuses its radio, and that radio lives in the
         buy box at the top of the page. Focusing an off-screen control
         makes the browser scroll it into view, so a tap down here threw
         the shopper back up the page (measured: scrollY 2630 -> 402, with
         no scrollTo call anywhere — it was the focus, and document height
         never changed).
         'change' is what the picker actually binds
         (assets/variant-picker.js:46, delegated on <variant-picker>), and
         its handler reads event.target, so dispatching on the radio gives
         it exactly what a real change would. Verified: scroll holds and
         activeElement never leaves <body>. */
      radios[i].checked = true;
      radios[i].dispatchEvent(new Event('change', { bubbles: true }));
      return;
    }
  }

  /* Picker -> strip. */
  function onVariantUpdate(e) {
    var v = e && e.detail ? e.detail.resource : null;
    if (!v || !v.id) return;
    var id = String(v.id);
    /* URL reconcile — a belt for a rare stock fluke. The picker defers its
       ?variant= writes (yieldToMainThread -> replaceState), and under
       main-thread contention from rapidly aborted changes they can land
       out of order: observed once 2026-08-24 — URL held Medium while the
       form, strip and cart all settled on Large, so a refresh or a shared
       link would reopen the wrong size. Two instrumented reproductions of
       the same sequence came out FIFO-correct, so this can't be chased —
       but settle IS ground truth, so on every settle the URL is made to
       agree with it. Only when a variant param already exists: a bare URL
       stays bare, we never add state the shopper didn't create. */
    try {
      var u = new URL(window.location.href);
      var uv = u.searchParams.get('variant');
      if (uv && uv !== id) {
        u.searchParams.set('variant', id);
        history.replaceState(history.state, '', u.toString());
      }
    } catch (err) { /* cosmetic guard — never fatal */ }
    for (var k in DATA) {
      if (DATA[k] && String(DATA[k].variant) === id) {
        if (k !== current) select(k, { fromPicker: true });
        return;
      }
    }
  }
  document.addEventListener('variant:update', onVariantUpdate);

  /* This listener is on `document`, so unlike everything else in this
     component it does NOT die with the section's DOM. The theme editor
     mounts and unmounts sections all day; without this the listeners
     stack up and one picker change re-runs select() N times. */
  document.addEventListener('shopify:section:unload', function onSyncUnload(e) {
    if (e.detail && e.detail.sectionId !== ROOT.dataset.sectionId) return;
    document.removeEventListener('variant:update', onVariantUpdate);
    document.removeEventListener('shopify:section:unload', onSyncUnload);
  });

  /* Point the BUY row at the size that is currently lit. */
  function syncBuy(d) {
    if (!buyEl) return;
    /* A size with no variant behind it (product ships fewer than three)
       hides the row rather than leaving the previous size's price sitting
       under a button that would add a different mirror. */
    if (!d.variant) { buyEl.hidden = true; return; }
    buyEl.hidden = false;

    if (priceEl) priceEl.textContent = d.price_html || '';
    if (wasEl) {
      var w = d.was_html || '';
      wasEl.textContent = w;
      wasEl.hidden = !w;
    }
    if (!atcEl) return;

    atcEl.setAttribute('data-casa-sz-atc', d.variant);
    if (d.url) atcEl.setAttribute('href', d.url);

    var avail = d.available !== false;
    if (avail) {
      atcEl.removeAttribute('data-soldout');
      atcEl.removeAttribute('aria-disabled');
    } else {
      atcEl.setAttribute('data-soldout', '1');
      atcEl.setAttribute('aria-disabled', 'true');
    }
    /* Only touch the label while the button is idle. Mid-add it is showing
       "Adding…" / "Added ✓" under its own timer, and writing here would
       blank the feedback the shopper is reading. */
    if (atcEl.getAttribute('aria-busy') !== 'true' && atcLabelEl) {
      atcLabelEl.textContent = avail ? 'Add to cart' : 'Sold out';
    }
  }

  function select(k, opts) {
    var d = DATA[k];
    if (!d) return;
    syncBuy(d);
    /* A select() that CAME from the variant picker must not push back to it,
       or the two sit in a loop trading 1 MB page fetches. */
    if (!(opts && opts.fromPicker)) pushToPicker(k);
    nameEl.textContent = d.name;
    inchesEl.textContent = d.inches;
    if (hInEl)  hInEl.textContent  = d.h_in;
    if (dInEl)  dInEl.textContent  = d.depth_in;
    cmEl.textContent = d.cm;
    if (hCmEl)  hCmEl.textContent  = d.h_cm;
    if (dCmEl)  dCmEl.textContent  = d.depth_cm;
    useEl.textContent = d.use;
    popEl.hidden = !d.popular;
    mirrors.forEach(function (m) {
      m.classList.toggle('is-on', m.dataset.casaSzMirror === k);
    });
    chips.forEach(function (c) {
      c.classList.toggle('is-on', c.dataset.casaSzChip === k);
    });
    lits.forEach(function (el) {
      el.classList.toggle('is-on', el.dataset.casaSzLit === k);
    });
    if (bkHEl) bkHEl.textContent = d.h_in + '″';
    if (bkWEl) bkWEl.textContent = d.inches + '″';
    if (depthPlEl) depthPlEl.textContent = d.depth_in;
    placeBracket(k);
    current = k;
  }

  mirrors.forEach(function (m) {
    m.addEventListener('click', function () { select(m.dataset.casaSzMirror); });
  });
  chips.forEach(function (c) {
    c.addEventListener('click', function () { select(c.dataset.casaSzChip); });
  });
  hots.forEach(function (h) {
    h.addEventListener('click', function () { select(h.dataset.casaSzHot); });
  });

  /* ── Add to cart ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Ported from sections/casa-sizing-cube.liquid, which has been serving
     this exact contract on the Cube PDP. The <a href> is the no-JS path;
     everything below is the enhancement over it.

     The listener is on ROOT, not document, so it is torn down with the
     section on a theme-editor unmount — no global to leak and nothing to
     clean up in an unload handler. */
  if (atcEl) {
    /* This is a static "javascript" block — no Liquid runs here, so routes
       come off the root as data-*. Locale-prefixed storefronts serve
       /en-de/cart/add, so the literal "/cart/add.js" is wrong there. */
    var CART_ADD_URL = ROOT.dataset.cartAddUrl || '/cart/add';
    var CART_URL = ROOT.dataset.cartUrl || '/cart';

    /* Ask for the cart + header sections back in the same round-trip, so
       the drawer and the cart-count bubble are already re-rendered by the
       time the drawer opens. */
    function collectSectionIds() {
      var ids = {};
      document.querySelectorAll('[data-section-id], [id^="shopify-section-"]').forEach(function (el) {
        var sid = el.dataset.sectionId || (el.id && el.id.replace(/^shopify-section-/, ''));
        if (!sid) return;
        var tag = (el.tagName || '').toLowerCase();
        var cls = (el.className || '').toString();
        if (/cart/i.test(tag) || /cart/i.test(cls) || /header/i.test(cls)) ids[sid] = 1;
      });
      return Object.keys(ids);
    }

    /* The theme has been through Horizon and Dawn cart drawers and the
       merchant can swap the cart app again, so this probes rather than
       assumes. Falling through to /cart is the floor — the item is
       already added by this point, so the worst case is a page load, not
       a lost add. */
    function openAnyDrawer() {
      var horizon = document.querySelector('cart-drawer-component');
      if (horizon) {
        try { if (typeof horizon.open === 'function') { horizon.open(); return true; } } catch (e) {}
        try { if (typeof horizon.showDialog === 'function') { horizon.showDialog(); return true; } } catch (e) {}
      }
      var dawn = document.querySelector('cart-drawer, #cart-drawer, #CartDrawer');
      if (dawn) {
        try { if (typeof dawn.open === 'function') { dawn.open(); return true; } } catch (e) {}
        try { if (typeof dawn.show === 'function') { dawn.show(); return true; } } catch (e) {}
        dawn.classList.add('active', 'is-open', 'animate');
        dawn.classList.remove('hidden');
        dawn.setAttribute('open', '');
        if (dawn.tagName.toLowerCase() === 'dialog') {
          try { dawn.showModal(); return true; } catch (e) {}
        }
        return true;
      }
      var cartBtn = document.querySelector('.casa-header__cart-btn, [aria-label*="cart" i][role="button"], [aria-label*="cart" i].header__icon, a[href$="/cart"]');
      if (cartBtn) { try { cartBtn.click(); return true; } catch (e) {} }
      return false;
    }

    function broadcastCartUpdate(response, variantId) {
      var detail = {
        resource: response,
        sourceId: 'casa-sz-' + (ROOT.dataset.sectionId || ''),
        data: {
          source: 'casa-sizing',
          itemCount: 1,
          variantId: variantId,
          sections: response && response.sections ? response.sections : {}
        }
      };
      ['cart:update', 'cart:refresh', 'cart-update', 'cart:item-added', 'cart-drawer:refresh'].forEach(function (name) {
        try { document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent(name, { bubbles: true, detail: detail })); } catch (e) {}
      });
    }

    ROOT.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
      var cta = e.target.closest('[data-casa-sz-atc]');
      if (!cta || !ROOT.contains(cta)) return;

      /* Sold out keeps its href for the no-JS path, so the refusal has to
         happen here — otherwise a keyboard Enter on the greyed button
         navigates away. */
      if (cta.hasAttribute('data-soldout')) { e.preventDefault(); return; }

      var vid = cta.getAttribute('data-casa-sz-atc');
      if (!/^\d+$/.test(vid)) return; /* invalid — let the href navigate */

      e.preventDefault();
      if (cta.getAttribute('aria-busy') === 'true') return;
      cta.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');

      var labelEl = cta.querySelector('.casa-sz__atc-label');
      var orig = labelEl ? labelEl.textContent : '';
      function setLabel(t) { if (labelEl) labelEl.textContent = (t == null ? orig : t); }
      setLabel('Adding…');

      var sectionIds = collectSectionIds();
      var payload = { items: [{ id: parseInt(vid, 10), quantity: 1 }] };
      if (sectionIds.length) payload.sections = sectionIds.join(',');

      fetch(CART_ADD_URL + '.js', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify(payload),
        headers: {
          'Accept': 'application/json',
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
          'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest'
        }
      })
        .then(function (res) { if (!res.ok && res.status !== 422) throw new Error('network'); return res.json(); })
        .then(function (response) {
          /* 422 carries a real reason (out of stock, cart limit). Show
             Shopify's own message rather than a generic failure. */
          if (response && response.status) {
            setLabel(response.message || 'Unavailable');
            setTimeout(function () { setLabel(null); cta.removeAttribute('aria-busy'); }, 2200);
            return;
          }
          broadcastCartUpdate(response, vid);
          setLabel('Added ✓');
          setTimeout(function () {
            if (!openAnyDrawer()) { window.location.href = CART_URL; }
          }, 60);
          setTimeout(function () { setLabel(null); cta.removeAttribute('aria-busy'); }, 1500);
        })
        .catch(function () {
          /* Network died. Fall back to the href the button always had —
             the shopper lands on the PDP with this size selected. */
          cta.removeAttribute('aria-busy');
          setLabel(null);
          window.location.href = cta.getAttribute('href');
        });
    });
  }

  // The tunnel's hue drift is `infinite`, so left ungated it keeps
  // compositing a filter while the shopper is three sections away and
  // eats scroll frame budget. Only let it run while the plate is on
  // screen. No observer support -> leave it running (the old behaviour).
  if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
    var plate = ROOT.querySelector('[data-casa-sz-plate]');
    if (plate) {
      var driftIO = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
        entries.forEach(function (e) { ROOT.classList.toggle('is-live', e.isIntersecting); });
      }, { rootMargin: '120px 0px' });
      driftIO.observe(plate);
      document.addEventListener('shopify:section:unload', function onUnload(e) {
        if (e.detail && e.detail.sectionId !== ROOT.dataset.sectionId) return;
        driftIO.disconnect();
        document.removeEventListener('shopify:section:unload', onUnload);
      });
    }
  } else {
    ROOT.classList.add('is-live');
  }

  // The bracket is written in px, so it has to be re-placed whenever the
  // plate reflows. Observing the PLATE rather than the window because the
  // plate is a grid column — it changes width when the copy column wraps,
  // with no viewport change at all. Safe against a resize loop: everything
  // the callback moves is absolutely positioned inside the field, so it
  // cannot feed back into the plate's own size.
  if (bracketEl && plateEl && 'ResizeObserver' in window) {
    // isConnected guard: shopify:section:unload covers the theme editor, but
    // a Section Rendering API swap or an app rewriting the DOM replaces the
    // node without firing it, leaving this observer holding a detached target
    // forever. Self-heal on the first delivery after that happens.
    var bracketRO = new ResizeObserver(function () {
      if (!ROOT.isConnected) { bracketRO.disconnect(); return; }
      placeBracket(current);
    });
    bracketRO.observe(plateEl);
    document.addEventListener('shopify:section:unload', function onBracketUnload(e) {
      if (e.detail && e.detail.sectionId !== ROOT.dataset.sectionId) return;
      bracketRO.disconnect();
      document.removeEventListener('shopify:section:unload', onBracketUnload);
    });
  }



  /* fromPicker: the server already rendered this size BECAUSE the buy box
     has it selected. Pushing here would fire a page fetch on every load. */
  select(ROOT.dataset.default || 'L', { fromPicker: true });
}

function casaSzInitAll(scope) {
  (scope || document).querySelectorAll('.casa-sz').forEach(casaSzInit);
}

if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () { casaSzInitAll(); });
} else {
  casaSzInitAll();
}
document.addEventListener('shopify:section:load', function (e) {
  if (e.target) casaSzInitAll(e.target);
});
{% endjavascript %}

